    "status": "draft",
    "body": "In March 2022, Instagram introduced two ways of viewing the feed: Following, which displayed posts from followed accounts in chronological order, and Favorites, which showed recent posts from a user-selected list (Mosseri, 2022a). Yet opening the app still brought users to the algorithmically ranked Home feed; neither alternative could be set as the default (Perez, 2022). The distinction is subtle but consequential. Choosing whom to follow did not necessarily mean choosing which posts appeared first, while selecting an alternative view did not change the platform’s default arrangement. Here, “control” is divided across different decisions: selecting sources, ranking content, and defining the interface people first encounter. Why did these decisions become bundled inside a platform, and what other arrangements for distributing information have existed?\n\nThis essay examines Really Simple Syndication (RSS), a web feed format that enables publishers to distribute structured updates for aggregation by reader software (RSS Advisory Board, 2003). Rather than treating RSS as a primitive version of today’s algorithmic feeds, it asks how the technical, institutional, and cultural conditions of web syndication made subscription-based distribution possible, and where users’ control over sources ended. Google Reader serves as a historical case of RSS in practice; Instagram’s 2022 feed options provide a contemporary comparison, not a direct successor. Following a media-archaeological approach (Parikka, 2012; Huhtamo & Parikka, 2011), the essay examines technical specifications, archived interfaces, company announcements, and earlier distribution arrangements. It traces historical conditions before returning to the present to question whether newer feeds necessarily offer greater agency.",
