    "status": "draft",
    "body": "Before a reader could receive an update, someone had to produce, preserve, and transport a physical record. The Dunhuang Star Chart, held by the British Library as Or.8210/S.3326, offers a material example. Catalogued to 649–684 CE, the ink-on-paper manuscript measures 24.4 by 330 centimetres and contains hand-drawn constellations and Chinese annotations (International Dunhuang Programme [IDP], n.d.). Its long scroll required paper, drawing labour, and space for storage and consultation. Information could survive beyond its maker, but reaching another place or audience still depended on moving the object, granting access to it, or making another copy. The chart’s preservation at the Mogao Caves and its later collection by the British Library also remind us that survival and present-day visibility reflect institutional histories (Swift, 2010). We cannot infer its original readership from the manuscript alone. What this object reveals is a distribution problem, not a primitive version of RSS: how to extend access without requiring every reader to encounter the same physical artefact.",
