    "id": "chapter-2",
    "title": "Reproducing Information at Scale: Copies Need Institutions",
    "status": "draft",
    "body": "The Tripitaka Koreana exposes the infrastructure behind reproducible texts. UNESCO records 81,258 woodblocks carved in thirteenth-century Goryeo Korea under dynastic commission and preserved at Haeinsa Monastery (UNESCO, n.d.). A carved block could print a page repeatedly, but that possibility depended on wood, skilled carving, textual checking, storage, and fresh paper and labour for each impression. UNESCO describes how the monastery printed copies for scholarship and monastic education. The number of blocks indicates the scale of this undertaking, not the number of readers or the extent of public access. Nor does their survival establish where particular copies travelled in the thirteenth century. The collection therefore reveals a distinction crucial to information distribution: the technical ability to reproduce a text does not, by itself, determine who can obtain it. Institutions organize both reproduction and access.\n\nEuropean letterpress printing rearranged these constraints through different materials and markets; it was not the inevitable successor to Korean woodblock printing. Type still required composition, paper, labour, and channels connecting printers with buyers. Pettegree (2010) shows that early print depended on patrons, religious demand, commercial risk, and changing markets. Johns (1998) treats the credibility of printed knowledge as something produced through social practices, rather than automatically guaranteed by identical copies. A printing press displayed in Mainz’s Gutenberg Museum makes this work visible, although the photograph dates from 2019, not the fifteenth century. As newspaper demand expanded, presses were redesigned for speed, from hand-operated machines to mechanized rotary systems (Colclough, 2022). Greater output nevertheless left problems of collecting information, financing distribution, and reaching dispersed readers. Such costs help explain why later news organizations developed cooperative networks.",
