    "id": "chapter-3",
    "title": "The Rise of Content Syndication: Sharing the Cost of News",
    "status": "draft",
    "body": "In May 1846, five New York newspapers formed the Associated Press (AP) to share the expense of reporting the Mexican–American War (Library of Congress, n.d.). This arrangement addressed a problem of coordination and cost: correspondents had to gather information, messages had to travel, and each newspaper still had to publish its own edition. The electric telegraph accelerated transmission without eliminating those tasks. Studying American newspapers in the 1840s, Wang (2025) finds that telegraph access reduced the delay of Washington news and increased national political coverage in small-town papers. A surviving Morse receiver associated with an 1846 patent makes the technical infrastructure tangible, but its existence does not explain the AP’s cooperative rules (Smithsonian Institution, n.d.). The historical change was therefore neither purely mechanical nor purely editorial: faster communication became valuable when publishers organized and financed shared news collection. Speed enabled a network; institutions made it usable.\n\nOne-to-many news distribution took several organizational forms. The AP was a cooperative news agency serving member papers, whereas commercial feature syndicates sold columns, fiction, and comics to subscribing newspapers; chains, by contrast, joined newspapers through common ownership (Guarneri, 2019; University of Illinois Library, n.d.-a). The distinctions matter because each arrangement distributed authority differently. Reporters produced accounts; agencies or syndicates selected, packaged, and delivered material; newspaper editors decided what appeared, where it appeared, and sometimes how wire copy was shortened. Readers encountered the edited page, not the original dispatch (University of Illinois Library, n.d.-b). Commercial terms could constrain that apparent local discretion: syndicates licensed sought-after features and sometimes controlled publication dates (Guarneri, 2017, 2019). News agencies were not neutral pipes either. Nalbach (2003) documents how nineteenth-century international wire services depended on governments, financiers, and telegraph infrastructure. Shared distribution reduced duplicated effort while concentrating influence upstream.\n\nThe surviving documents reveal the difference between a circulating report and its printed destination. A typed AP bulletin dated May 7, 1915, recorded Washington’s reaction to the sinking of the Lusitania at 2:09 p.m.; the Library of Congress reproduces the dispatch in its history of the Washington bureau (Tucker, 2019). In a separate example, the June 26, 1922, front page of Washington’s Evening Star identifies the newspaper as an AP member and attributes several reports to the agency (Evening Star, 1922). These sources demonstrate agency copy and local newspaper presentation, not that the same particular story appeared identically in two papers. Subscription-based RSS later offered another way for one publisher’s updates to reach multiple readers, but RSS feeds do not require an AP-style newsroom, membership contract, or print editor. The useful comparison is structural: distribution separates content production from the decisions made at its destination.",
