    "id": "chapter-4",
    "title": "Before RSS: Competing Web Channels",
    "status": "draft",
    "body": "By 1997, repeated visits to websites had become a problem of finding and checking updates. WIRED forecast that new push services might even displace the web browser: information would arrive without users repeatedly following links (Kelly & Wolf, 1997). PointCast, Microsoft's proposed Active Channels, and other products offered different versions of that promise. Microsoft promoted Channel Definition Format (CDF) as a way for publishers to automate desktop delivery (Microsoft, 1997). Their common appeal was not merely speed but relief from the labour of checking. This echoes Huhtamo's (2011) account of recurring media topoi: familiar wishes for effortless access reappear in new technical settings. Yet the headline was an enthusiastic contemporary prediction, not evidence that browsing would end. Web pages, commercial push services, and experimental syndication formats overlapped rather than forming an inevitable path to RSS (RSS Advisory Board, n.d.).\n\nMicrosoft's Channel Definition Format made the promise of automation technically inspectable. The 1997 submission to W3C described a user choosing a channel once, after which compatible receiver software could retrieve updates on a schedule (Ellerman, 1997). In its illustrative XML, <Channel> describes a collection, <Item> points to a resource, and <Schedule> with <IntervalTime> specifies update intervals. These labels organized information about remote content: the file alone did not deliver a broadcast. Publishers still needed web servers; recipients needed compatible programs. The document also allowed tracking parameters, a reminder that an open channel description was compatible with commercial monitoring (Ellerman, 1997). Microsoft's announcement promised simple publishing and access to large audiences, but this was a vendor's advocacy rather than proof of universal adoption (Microsoft, 1997). Nor should CDF be treated as a direct technical ancestor of RSS.\n\nPointCast was a service and interface, not simply a file format. A contemporaneous review of its Japanese beta shows selectable channels, timestamped headlines, a scrolling news ticker, and prominent advertising (Higuchi & Kobayashi, 1997). Its controlled presentation combined consumer choice with service-provider packaging. In 1997, PointCast 2.0 also introduced CDF-supported connections and changes intended to reduce bandwidth use (Helft, 1997): even a relatively open channel format could operate within a proprietary product. By 2000, WIRED reported PointCast's closure after its commercial momentum had faded (Bicknell, 2000). That later account is evidence of a changed business outcome, not a complete causal explanation. Network capacity, advertisers, providers, and users all mattered. This abandoned configuration of subscriptions, desktop presentation, and funding exposes an alternative future: automatic updates did not disappear, but this way of organizing them failed to remain dominant.",
