      "PointCast was a service and interface, not simply a file format. A contemporaneous review of its Japanese beta shows selectable channels, timestamped headlines, a scrolling news ticker, and prominent advertising (Higuchi & Kobayashi, 1997). Its controlled presentation combined consumer choice with service-provider packaging. In 1997, PointCast 2.0 also introduced CDF-supported connections and changes intended to reduce bandwidth use (Helft, 1997): even a relatively open channel format could operate within a proprietary product. By 2000, WIRED reported PointCast's closure after its commercial momentum had faded (Bicknell, 2000). That later account is evidence of a changed business outcome, not a complete causal explanation. Network capacity, advertisers, providers, and users all mattered. This abandoned configuration of subscriptions, desktop presentation, and funding exposes an alternative future: automatic updates did not disappear, but this way of organizing them failed to remain dominant."
