    "title": "RSS and Google Reader: Subscription Is Not the Same as Control",
    "status": "draft",
    "body": "The 1999 RSS 0.90 specification was not simply a declaration of independence from portals. Netscape’s My Netscape Network (MNN) invited publishers to prepare an RDF Site Summary (.rdf), register its address with Netscape, and place an “Add Channel” button on their sites (Netscape Communications Corporation, 1999). The format made headlines machine-readable, but participation in MNN remained organized through a particular service. Its limits were concrete: one channel per Netcenter account, one channel per file, no more than 15 items, and an 8-kilobyte file ceiling. Netscape’s help pages supplied further instructions for keeping channels valid (Netscape Communications Corporation, n.d.). Here, source description and distribution access were distinct. RSS offered a reusable way to express updates, but readers’ discovery of those updates could still depend on a portal.\n\nRSS 2.0 later made the separation clearer. In its 2003 specification, a publisher provides an XML document containing a <channel> with a title, link, description, and <item> entries pointing to individual updates (Winer, 2003). A reader stores the addresses of selected feeds, requests them periodically from publishers’ servers, detects changes, and assembles a reading view. The protocol defines content fields, not an obligatory display order or recommendation algorithm. Nor is delivery free of infrastructure costs: a study of approximately 100,000 feeds found widely varying update intervals, meaning clients must trade freshness against repeated requests (Liu et al., 2005). The familiar phrase “content comes to you” therefore hides a coordinated sequence of server publication, network retrieval, client polling, and interface presentation. These steps create opportunities for both choice and mediation.\n\nFor a subscriber, this arrangement offered meaningful but bounded agency. A user could choose the publishers to follow and organize feeds rather than relying entirely on a newspaper’s assembled page or a portal’s front page. Yet four separate questions follow: who selects sources, who orders items, who maintains the reading software, and whether subscriptions can be moved elsewhere. Netscape’s registration process had already shown that a portable description format could coexist with a controlled discovery channel (Netscape Communications Corporation, 1999). Google Reader, introduced in October 2005, provided searching, multimedia views, sharing, and algorithm-assisted prioritization within one hosted service (Google, 2005). The user's choice of feed addresses did not give them ownership of the reader or every rule governing the experience. Control was distributed across technical and institutional layers.\n\nThe 2006 Reader archive makes a simple “RSS versus algorithms” opposition untenable. The Google Reader team explained that users could sort their reading lists “by date” or “automatically,” with the latter surfacing more relevant items (Parparita, 2006). Until July 2006, the default had been automatic ordering with previously read items visible. Responding to complaints that new posts were not always at the top, Google switched the defaults to date ordering and hidden read items. A surviving screenshot shows both switches. This case distinguishes three powers: choosing a subscription, selecting an available sorting mode, and setting the initial defaults most people encounter. Importantly, the source-selection technology did not change when Google altered the interface. The episode is evidence not of complete user sovereignty, but of contested product-level decisions shaped in part by user feedback.\n\nThe contest continued in 2009. Google introduced a “magic” sorting option that ranked items using a reader’s own activity and wider Reader usage, alongside a “Popular items” view of highly circulated material (Yang, 2009). Crucially, magic sort was an option, not a requirement that every RSS item be ranked in this way. The announcement describes the signals at a broad level; it does not disclose complete model weights or individual ranking outcomes. Gillespie (2014) helps explain why this matters: what counts as relevant is a social and institutional judgment embedded in an apparently technical ordering. Bucher (2018) likewise directs attention to how algorithms organize visibility. An item could come from a source the user had explicitly chosen yet still appear farther down the screen because of the reader’s sorting policy.\n\nIn 2011, the platform also reorganized how subscribed material travelled socially. A Reader redesign replaced its built-in “Like,” “Share,” and “Share with Note” functions with Google+ interactions, including +1 and sharing with circles (Green, 2011). Subscription to a publisher’s feed and circulation of a story among contacts were therefore different activities, even when they occurred around the same item. The change shows that a relatively stable feed standard could be surrounded by shifting social interfaces. It also narrowed some existing pathways for Reader communities and created new dependencies on another Google product. Such changes fit a broader distinction between distribution formats and platform governance: ownership of the reader allowed Google to redefine social participation. The 2011 announcement documents a product decision, however, not a sufficient explanation for the service's later closure.\n\nOn March 13, 2013, Google announced that Reader would be retired on July 1. Its public statement cited declining use and a company-wide focus on fewer products; the Reader team said subscriptions and other data could be exported with Google Takeout (Hölzle, 2013; Green, 2013). Export preserved a route to leave but not a vote on whether the service would remain online. The contrast exposes two forms of agency that should not be conflated: portability of subscription data and authority over a hosted infrastructure. Meanwhile, the RSS Advisory Board continued publishing a maintained RSS 2.0 specification beyond the life of Reader (RSS Advisory Board, 2009). The 2013 shutdown therefore marked the end of one widely used interface and institution, not the disappearance of RSS as a format. What users did not fully choose was not only which story appeared first, but who could keep their means of reading available.",
