    "id": "chapter-6",
    "title": "Algorithmic Feeds: An Alternative, Not an Inevitable Upgrade",
    "status": "draft",
    "body": "To compare RSS with contemporary feeds without assuming a linear progression, I return to Instagram as documented in early 2022. In March, Meta introduced Following, a chronological view of posts from followed accounts, and Favorites, a chronological view from up to fifty selected accounts (Mosseri, 2022a). The standard Home feed, however, still combined followed accounts with suggested content, while Favorites posts could also be promoted within Home. This coexistence is analytically useful: one platform offered both explicitly selected streams and platform-organized visibility. Meta framed these options as ways to give people control, but a product announcement establishes available functions, not whether they increased news diversity or changed everyday habits. Instagram here is a bounded comparison of interface rules, not the inevitable successor to RSS.\n\nMeta's Instagram Feed Ranking System Card, last updated February 23, 2022, exposes a different layer of choice (Meta, 2022). In the ranked Feed described there, the system gathers eligible posts from followed accounts, removes rule-breaking content, and predicts interactions such as likes, saves, and profile taps. It combines those predictions into scores, applies demotions and diversity rules, and orders posts before display. These operations do not merely transmit a publisher's update: they allocate relative visibility through platform-defined criteria. Gillespie (2014) draws attention to the politics of relevance; Bucher (2018) examines power relations embedded in computational selection. The card is nevertheless a partial, company-authored description of one Feed system. It does not reveal full model parameters, cover every Instagram surface, or establish how individuals actually experienced their feeds.\n\nBeing able to switch views was not the same as deciding the default arrangement. Following and Favorites were accessible through the Instagram menu, but neither could be set as the default Home feed at launch (Perez, 2022). Google Reader's July 2006 update also distinguished chronological from automatic ordering, yet it changed the default after receiving user complaints (Parparita, 2006). Both cases separate three decisions: which sources to select, which ranking modes are available, and which mode appears first. Their governance still differed. Reader sorted explicitly subscribed feeds within a hosted aggregator; Instagram offered chronological alternatives alongside a ranked Home that could include suggested posts. This comparison cannot establish which system produced a more diverse information diet. It identifies the places users could intervene—and the power companies retained over the starting point.\n\nResearch complicates any picture of completely passive users. Karlsson et al. (2022) distinguish publishing, social sharing, and algorithmic spreading as gatekeeping logics; their empirical case concerns Facebook news circulation, not Instagram's 2022 Feed. Merten (2021) finds that people actively curate social-media news by following, hiding, or blocking sources, although her survey likewise does not measure use of Instagram's new views. Bodó (2019) shows that news organizations' personalization goals can differ from platforms' commercial imperatives, while Mitova et al. (2023) caution against treating the effects of news recommenders as settled. The historical comparison is therefore not a pure RSS past versus a manipulated present. In both arrangements, readers acted within infrastructures they did not design. What shifted was how source selection, ranking, and control of the interface were distributed.",
