    "id": "chapter-7",
    "title": "Who Controls the Feed? Reassembling a History of Selection",
    "status": "draft",
    "body": "The preceding archive does not trace a single invention handed from newspapers to RSS and then Instagram. It reveals recurring tasks allocated to different actors. The Associated Press made news-gathering costs shareable among member newspapers, while local editors still selected what to print (Library of Congress, n.d.; University of Illinois Library, n.d.). PointCast packaged channels inside a commercial desktop service, whereas Microsoft's Channel Definition Format specified scheduled retrieval without deciding how every receiver would display content (Ellerman, 1997; Higuchi & Kobayashi, 1997). RSS standardized publishers' update descriptions, but reading software still assembled the visible stream (Netscape Communications Corporation, 1999; Winer, 2003). Instagram's 2022 Feed made ranking an explicitly platform-managed operation (Meta, 2022). These arrangements are comparable by function—production, transport, and selection—not as stages of direct technical descent.\n\nControl therefore cannot be reduced to a button labelled “subscribe.” Four questions require separate answers: who chooses sources, who orders items, who sets the initial view, and who can preserve or migrate the service? RSS specifies content fields, not a mandatory display order (Winer, 2003). Google Reader offered automatic prioritization at launch, altered its defaults after complaints in 2006, and later introduced optional personalized ranking (Google, 2005; Parparita, 2006; Yang, 2009). Its users could export subscriptions, but could not prevent the hosted service's retirement (Green, 2013). Instagram offered chronological alternatives in 2022 without letting users make them the default Home view (Mosseri, 2022a; Perez, 2022). Protocol, reader, and platform are not interchangeable units of analysis.\n\nAbandoned products make this distinction especially visible. In 1997, push-media advocates anticipated automatic delivery replacing deliberate browsing (Kelly & Wolf, 1997); by 2000, PointCast's commercial service was closing (Bicknell, 2000). Google Reader, built around RSS subscriptions, was retired in 2013, with Google providing an export route for reader data (Green, 2013). A contemporary screenshot captures its shutdown warning as users saw it, not Google's internal decision-making (Rodriguez, 2013). Yet the RSS specification remained available and maintained separately from that product (RSS Advisory Board, 2009). These outcomes did not share one demonstrable cause: PointCast's advertising and bandwidth conditions differ from Google's portfolio decisions. Following Parikka (2012) and Huhtamo (2011), their value to media archaeology lies in the unrealized possibilities they expose. The desire for timely, automatically gathered information survived particular interfaces, contracts, and businesses.\n\nThe strongest counterexamples prevent a nostalgic conclusion. Google Reader was already capable of algorithm-assisted selection in 2005, while Instagram offered chronological views in 2022 (Google, 2005; Mosseri, 2022a). RSS was not a world without mediation, and ranking platforms do not eliminate every deliberate choice. Rather, information distribution repeatedly redistributes authority among publishers, infrastructure operators, software designers, intermediaries, and readers. The decisive historical question is not whether a newer feed is technically superior, but which decisions users can inspect, revise, carry elsewhere, or contest—and which remain contingent on a service provider. This framework offers a more precise account than a progress narrative because it explains both the persistence of subscriptions and the recurrent limits of user agency.",
