    "title": "The Alternatives That Remain",
    "status": "draft",
    "body": "The history assembled here does not show a straight passage from print to RSS to algorithmic feeds. Different societies needed ways to reproduce, coordinate, and select information, but the solutions depended on available materials, network costs, institutions, and commercial priorities. News agencies pooled reporting, early Web channels promised automatic delivery, and RSS described updates in a reusable format; none of these arrangements alone determined what readers ultimately saw (Ellerman, 1997; Netscape Communications Corporation, 1999; Winer, 2003). RSS made it easier to subscribe to chosen sources, yet the interface remained a further site of decision. Google Reader’s adjustable sorting and eventual shutdown make the distinction concrete: control over subscriptions was not equivalent to control over ranking or the service’s future (Green, 2013; Parparita, 2006). Instagram’s 2022 chronological alternatives likewise existed alongside a platform-ranked Home feed (Mosseri, 2022a). The central finding is therefore a recurring redistribution of authority, not a steady increase or decline in user freedom.\n\nThis interpretation also has limits. The archive privileges surviving institutional documentation and examples from East Asia, Europe, and the United States; other media traditions and readers’ experiences remain underrepresented. Product announcements reveal what companies said their systems could do, not how every person used them. Meta’s account of Feed ranking is a partial explanation, and functional similarities across centuries cannot establish direct technological inheritance (Meta, 2022). These gaps matter because media archaeology is most useful when it resists treating today’s interfaces as history’s inevitable destination (Huhtamo, 2011; Parikka, 2012). The aim is not to reinstall RSS as a perfect alternative: early RSS could coexist with portals, and Google Reader itself incorporated automated ordering. Rather, the recovered alternatives sharpen the question raised by the opening Instagram interface. When we choose a source, what else have we chosen—and what remains decided for us? A meaningful choice of feed also depends on whether its ordering, defaults, and continuity can be understood, challenged, or changed.",
